    <!-- ─── FAQ ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="nfaq" id="faq">
      <div class="container">
        <header class="nfaq__head reveal">
          <span class="eyebrow">FAQ · Notes on Android</span>
          <h2>The four things people ask first.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            If your question is not here, the
            <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> covers the security model and the
            <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> lists every release.
          </p>
        </header>
        <ul class="nfaq__list" role="list">
          <li>
            <details class="nfaq__item" id="q-vs-evernote-notion-obsidian">
              <summary class="nfaq__q">
                <span class="nfaq__q-text">Is this Evernote? Notion? Obsidian?</span>
                <span class="nfaq__caret" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <div class="nfaq__body">
                <p class="nfaq__a">
                  Local-first like Obsidian. Voice-first like nothing else.
                  Free forever like Evernote once was, before it stopped being.
                  The data model is plain SQLite plus a markdown body, so it opens
                  in any tool you already use. The Android build is a Capacitor
                  shell around a web bundle, so the same code path runs in a
                  desktop browser too.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </li>
          <li>
            <details class="nfaq__item" id="q-read-messages">
              <summary class="nfaq__q">
                <span class="nfaq__q-text">Does Pulse read my messages?</span>
                <span class="nfaq__caret" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <div class="nfaq__body">
                <p class="nfaq__a">
                  Only what you choose to share. There is no SMS access, no
                  notification listener, no accessibility-service trick that
                  quietly scans other apps. The capture sheet opens only when you
                  tap "share to Pulse" in another app. That's the entire surface.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </li>
          <li>
            <details class="nfaq__item" id="q-sync-between-devices">
              <summary class="nfaq__q">
                <span class="nfaq__q-text">Can I sync notes between phone and desktop?</span>
                <span class="nfaq__caret" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <div class="nfaq__body">
                <p class="nfaq__a">
                  In v0.6.0, Pulse is single-device on each platform. Manual sync
                  works today: the <code>notes.db</code> file is plain SQLite, so
                  point Syncthing, a folder-sync tool, or a git client at the
                  app's data directory and you have free cross-device sync. Pro
                  tier (planned, $5/mo) will add opt-in end-to-end-encrypted
                  automatic sync.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </li>
          <li>
            <details class="nfaq__item" id="q-leave-pulse">
              <summary class="nfaq__q">
                <span class="nfaq__q-text">What if I want to leave?</span>
                <span class="nfaq__caret" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <div class="nfaq__body">
                <p class="nfaq__a">
                  Your <code>notes.db</code> is plain SQLite. The Android app data
                  directory on the device is accessible through a file manager
                  with root or via ADB backup. Open the file with any SQLite
                  browser, or export to a folder of <code>.md</code> files from
                  Settings → Data. No export cap, no premium-only feature, no
                  retention.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

